'\" t
.TH ROFFSUB 1 2026-11-15 "roffsub r0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
roffsub \- check, compile, and render terminal manuals written in roffsub
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBroffsub\fR [\fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIstyle\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fImeasure\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-e\fR [\fB\-s\fR \fIstyle\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-c\fR \fIfile\fR ...
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-i\fR [\fB\-d\fR \fIdate\fR] [\fB\-r\fR \fIrev\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-p\fR [\fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-g\fR \fBc\fR|\fBbash\fR \fIname\fR [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-n\fR [\fB\-d\fR \fIdate\fR] [\fB\-r\fR \fIrev\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
\fBroffsub\fR \fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-V\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B roffsub
works with manuals written in roffsub, a subset of the man macros and tbl that every roff program displays and that small awk and C routines can also render.
It renders a manual for the terminal in use, at that terminal's width, with bold and italic where the terminal can show them.
It also checks a manual's form, compiles it into records, formats it at a fixed width, and writes the text as a C array or a bash heredoc for a program to carry.
.PP
.B roffsub
comes as a C binary and as a bash function that behave alike.
The function serves systems where programs cannot be run from the user's own directories, such as a home directory mounted noexec, so one package works everywhere.
Both use awk to check, compile, and generate; the binary renders records and pre-formatted text without it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR
Render at \fIwidth\fR columns.
Without it the width comes from
.BR MANWIDTH ,
then from the terminal, then 80; output that is not a terminal is 80 columns unless
.B MANWIDTH
is set.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIstyle\fR
Write emphasis as
.B plain
(none),
.B overstrike
(backspace sequences for a pager),
.B sgr
(terminal control sequences), or
.B pre
(font escapes for later emission).
Without it the style follows the destination; see
.BR "OUTPUT CHOICES" .
.TP
\fB\-m\fR \fImeasure\fR
Never fill text past \fImeasure\fR columns, however wide the terminal.
.TP
.B \-e
Emit pre-formatted text, as written by
.BR \-p ,
in the chosen style.
.TP
.B \-c
Check the form of each \fIfile\fR and report every departure from roffsub as \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR: \fIid\fR: \fImessage\fR.
The exit status is 1 when an error was found.
With
.B ROFFSUB_STRICT
set, warnings count as errors too.
.TP
.B \-i
Write the compiled records of \fIfile\fR.
.B \-d
and
.B \-r
fill in
.B @DATE@
and
.B @VERSION@
on the
.B .TH
line.
.TP
.B \-p
Write \fIfile\fR formatted at a fixed width (default 80) with its font changes kept, for a program's
.B preformatted
profile.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR \fBc\fR|\fBbash\fR \fIname\fR
Write the lines of \fIfile\fR as a C array called \fIname\fR, or as a marked bash region that reads them into the variable \fIname\fR.
.TP
.B \-n
Write \fIfile\fR as the page to install for man.
.B \-d
and
.B \-r
fill in the
.B .TH
line as for
.BR \-i ,
and each table is rewritten so that roff draws it as roffsub does: no vertical lines, and a + where a rule meets a column gap.
The source itself stays as written.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show this manual.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.PP
A \fIfile\fR may be a roffsub source or its compiled records; records begin with the line
.BR v ,
a tab, and
.BR 1 .
Without a \fIfile\fR the standard input is read.
.SH OUTPUT CHOICES
.TS
tab(|);
lb0 c0 lb0 c0 lb0 c0 lb
l0 c0 l0 c0 l0 c0 l
l0 c0 l0 c0 l0 c0 l
l0 c0 l0 c0 l0 c0 l
l0 c0 l0 c0 l0 c0 l.
OUTPUT||GOES TO||STYLE||WIDTH
_|\-+\-|_|\-+\-|_|\-+\-|_
terminal||the pager||overstrike||terminal
terminal, PAGER is cat||the terminal||sgr||terminal
file or pipe||the file or pipe||plain||80
.TE
.PP
The pager is
.B PAGER
when set, otherwise
.B less
when installed, otherwise
.BR more .
.B TERM=dumb
selects plain.
Command-line options override the environment, and the environment overrides these defaults.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MANWIDTH
The rendering width.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The terminal width, when
.B MANWIDTH
is not set.
.TP
.B PAGER
The pager for terminal output.
.TP
.B ROFFSUB_STYLE
The style: plain, overstrike, sgr, or pre.
.TP
.B ROFFSUB_SGR
Control sequence parameters for bold and italic, as
.BR b=1;33:i=4;36 .
The defaults are 1 (bold) and 4 (underline).
Colors let bold and italic show as distinct colors.
.TP
.B ROFFSUB_MEASURE
The maximum line length.
.TP
.B ROFFSUB_HEADING
The number of columns section headings are moved in from the margin; 0 by default.
Everything else stays in place.
.TP
.B ROFFSUB_STRICT
When set, the check treats warnings as errors.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a check found errors, 2 on a usage or input error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a manual, then read it as a program would show it:
.PP
.nf
roffsub \-c srt.1
roffsub srt.1
roffsub \-w 50 \-s plain srt.1 > srt.txt
.fi
.PP
Embed the full manual in a C program and in a bash function:
.PP
.nf
roffsub \-i \-d 2026\-11\-15 \-r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.ir
roffsub \-g c srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.h
roffsub \-g bash _srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.bash
.fi
.PP
Write the page to install:
.PP
.nf
roffsub \-n \-d 2026\-11\-15 \-r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.1.out
.fi
.PP
Embed a pre-formatted manual in a small program:
.PP
.nf
roffsub \-p srt.1 > srt.pre
roffsub \-g c srt_man srt.pre > srt.man.h
.fi
.SH FILES
.TS
tab(|);
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx
lb0 c0 lx.
roffsub-check.awk||the form check
roffsub-ir.awk||T{
the compiler, source to records
T}
roffsub-render.awk||T{
the renderer, records to terminal text
T}
roffsub-emit.awk||T{
the emitter, pre-formatted text to terminal text
T}
roffsub-gen.awk||T{
embedding as a C array or bash region
T}
roffsub-man.awk||the page to install for man
roffsub.h||the renderer in C
roffsub-emit.h||the emitter in C
roffsub-tty.h||T{
width, style, and pager choice in C
T}
.TE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR roffsub (7),
.BR man (1),
.BR less (1)
